<section>
  <h2>The Central Analytical Red Thread</h2>
  <p>MPPA’s existential crisis in FY2020 stems from a dual structural trap:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Unprofitable Hypermarket Floorplates:</strong> 84.5% of revenue was concentrated in large-format Hypermarts (6,000–8,000 m²), which experienced -12.0% CAGR and a 22% COVID revenue collapse, while trapped in fixed-rent mall anchor leases under PSAK 73 (Rp 2,253.2B in lease liabilities, Note 19).</li>
    <li><strong>Constrained Debt Capacity:</strong> Negative operating cash flow and a widening working-capital deficit of Rp 1.23T prevent reliance on external bank borrowing, as doing so would breach Bank Negara Indonesia (BNI) credit covenants (Debt/Equity &lt; 2.5x, Interest Coverage &gt; 2.0x, Note 15).</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>The Strategic Solution:</strong> A 100% self-funded transformation deploying capital from lease restructuring and non-core divestments into high-yield, compact neighborhood supermarkets (Hyfresh) and on-demand dark stores.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Core Strategic Matrices (Fred R. David Stages 1–4)</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Stage 1: Input Matrices (CPM, EFE, IFE)</dt>
    <dd>Competitive Profile Matrix (CPM) benchmarks MPPA across 6 rivals (Indomaret, Alfamart, Transmart, Alfamidi, LotteMart). External Factor Evaluation (EFE = 2.68) confirms high external urgency. Internal Factor Evaluation (IFE = 2.36) identifies severe lease drag and negative liquidity, counterbalanced by 3 central cold-chain distribution centers.</dd>

    <dt>Stage 2: Matching Matrices (TOWS, SPACE, BCG, IE, GSM)</dt>
    <dd>SPACE Matrix places MPPA in the <em>Defensive Quadrant</em> (X: -1.0, Y: -0.6). BCG Matrix reveals Hypermart transitioning to Dog/Cash Cow while Hyfresh is a rapid-growth Star. IE Matrix plots Corporate MPPA in Cell V (Hold &amp; Maintain) while Hyfresh sits in Cell II (Grow &amp; Build). Grand Strategy Matrix (GSM) places Corporate in Quadrant III and Hyfresh in Quadrant II.</dd>

    <dt>Stage 3: Decision Stage (QSPM Matrix)</dt>
    <dd>Quantitative Strategic Planning Matrix (QSPM) evaluates the two primary alternatives. <strong>Strategy 1 (Compact Hyfresh Rollout &amp; 'Hy-' Master Brand Consolidation) wins decisively with a Total Attractiveness Score (TAS) of 6.620</strong> versus Strategy 2 (Hypermarket Downsizing into Dark Stores) with a TAS of 5.460 (+1.160 point lead).</dd>

    <dt>Stage 4: Recommendations &amp; Capital Budgeting (Slides 29–30)</dt>
    <dd>Strategy 1 is executed as the primary growth vector, while Strategy 2 is retained as the operational efficiency mechanism to liberate floor space and working capital. Strategy 3 restructures PSAK 73 leases and liquidates SmartClub wholesale and FMX kiosks to fund the expansion.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

