<div id="handoff-content">
<section id="handoff-section-0">
  <h2>Purpose &amp; Zero-Build Execution</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Purpose</dt>
    <dd>Comprehensive project record for Group 7’s Corporate Strategy case study on PT Matahari Putra Prima Tbk (MPPA). Serves as the primary onboarding handoff when moving across computers or starting a new AI session.</dd>
    <dt>Zero-Build Architecture</dt>
    <dd>The entire project runs directly in any web browser without Node.js, Vite, Webpack, or npm dependencies. Vue 3 standalone runtime is bundled locally in <code>assets/vue.global.prod.js</code>. The application functions completely offline.</dd>
    <dt>Execution Command</dt>
    <dd><code>open mppa-case-study.html</code> (macOS) or double-click the HTML file. In DevTools console: <code>window.setView('powerpoint')</code> to enter presentation mode, or <code>window.showSlide(29)</code> to jump directly to Slide 30.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-1">
  <h2>The Strategic Management Framework</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Textbook Methodology</dt>
    <dd>Strictly follows Fred R. David &amp; Forest R. David, <em>Strategic Management: A Competitive Advantage Approach, Concepts and Cases</em> (Pearson, 17th/18th Ed.).</dd>
    <dt>Stage 1: The Input Stage</dt>
    <dd>External Factor Evaluation (EFE), Internal Factor Evaluation (IFE), and Competitive Profile Matrix (CPM).</dd>
    <dt>Stage 2: The Matching Stage</dt>
    <dd>TOWS Matrix, Strategic Position and Action Evaluation (SPACE) Matrix, Boston Consulting Group (BCG) Matrix, Internal-External (IE) Matrix, and Grand Strategy Matrix (GSM).</dd>
    <dt>Stage 3: The Decision Stage</dt>
    <dd>Quantitative Strategic Planning Matrix (QSPM) determining the relative attractiveness of feasible alternative actions.</dd>
    <dt>Stage 4: Implementation Stage</dt>
    <dd>Actionable strategic recommendations, departmental execution roadmap, and audit-grade 5-year capital allocation / self-funding budgeting model.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-2">
  <h2>Complete 30-Slide Master Directory</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Slides 01–08: Corporate Diagnosis &amp; Baseline</dt>
    <dd>01 Overview · 02 Corporate History · 03 Retail Formats Portfolio · 04 Organizational Structure · 05 Vision &amp; Mission · 06 Background of the Study · 07 Statement of the Problem · 08 Revenue Mix Breakdown (Hypermart 84.5%).</dd>
    <dt>Slides 09–12: Physical &amp; Digital Footprint (2020)</dt>
    <dd>09 Geographic Store Footprint (611,490 m² across 73 cities) · 10 Logistics Network &amp; Cold-Chain DCs · 11 Digital Omnichannel Reach (GrabMart, Shopee, Tokopedia) · 12 COVID-19 Operating Disruption.</dd>
    <dt>Slides 13–18: External &amp; Competitive Analysis</dt>
    <dd>13 PESTLE Analysis Overview · 14 PESTLE Evidence &amp; Digital Shifts · 15 Porter’s Five Forces Overview · 16 Five Forces Grocery Industry Dynamics · 17 Five Main Competitors · 18 Competitive Profile Matrix (CPM).</dd>
    <dt>Slides 19–20: Audited Matrix Scores</dt>
    <dd>19 External Factor Evaluation (EFE Matrix = 2.68) · 20 Internal Factor Evaluation (IFE Matrix = 2.36).</dd>
    <dt>Slides 21–25: Strategic Formulation &amp; Matching</dt>
    <dd>21 TOWS / SWOT Strategy Matrix · 22 SPACE Matrix (Defensive Quadrant: X = -1.0, Y = -0.6) · 23 BCG Growth-Share Matrix · 24 Internal-External (IE) Matrix (Cell V &amp; Cell II) · 25 Grand Strategy Matrix (GSM Quadrant III &amp; II).</dd>
    <dt>Slides 26–30: Decision, Strategy &amp; Capital Budgeting</dt>
    <dd>26 Quantitative Strategic Planning Matrix (QSPM: Strat 1 TAS 6.620 vs Strat 2 TAS 5.460) · 27 Alternative Strategies Taxonomy · 28 Strategic Recommendations (The 3 Pillars) · 29 Implementation Roadmap · 30 Capital Allocation, Self-Funding Budget &amp; Store Rollout Expansion Map.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-3">
  <h2>Verified Financial &amp; Accounting Ledgers</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Revenue &amp; Liquidity Baseline</dt>
    <dd>FY2020 Net Sales: Rp 6,750.8B (-22.0% YoY). Operating Loss: Rp (581.4B). Current Assets: Rp 1,535.3B vs Current Liabilities: Rp 2,763.1B, creating a working capital deficit of Rp 1,227.8B (AR2020 Balance Sheet).</dd>
    <dt>PSAK 73 / IFRS 16 Lease Liabilities</dt>
    <dd>AR2020 Note 19: Long-term lease liabilities of Rp 2,253.2B. Note 28: Annual rental expense of Rp 166.2B. Renegotiating 15 anchor mall leases saves ~Rp 36B/year, totaling +Rp 180.0B over 5 years.</dd>
    <dt>BNI Credit Facilities &amp; Covenants</dt>
    <dd>AR2020 Note 15: PT Bank Negara Indonesia (Persero) Tbk credit facilities totaling Rp 500.0B. Covenants strictly require Interest Coverage &gt; 2.0x and Debt/Equity &lt; 2.5x. The 100% self-funded model prevents any new debt drawdowns, expanding Interest Coverage to 3.85x and driving Debt/Equity down to 0.45x.</dd>
    <dt>Non-Core Divestment Realizations</dt>
    <dd>Liquidating 2 SmartClub wholesale warehouses and closing 14 unprofitable FMX convenience kiosks yields +Rp 120.0B in net cash from inventory sell-through, deposit recoveries, and equipment salvage.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-4">
  <h2>Distribution Logistics &amp; Sourcing Hubs</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>3 Central Cold-Chain DCs (66,755 m²)</dt>
    <dd>1. Balaraja Central DC (31,500 m² - Banten/Jabodetabek, Halal cold storage). 2. Surabaya Regional DC (22,255 m² - East Java &amp; Bali). 3. Medan / Siborong-borong Hub (13,000 m² - North Sumatra).</dd>
    <dt>Trans-Java Logistics Spine</dt>
    <dd>Trans-Java tollway corridor connects Balaraja DC through Jakarta, Bandung, Cirebon, Semarang, Solo, and Surabaya, enabling sub-4 hour store replenishment.</dd>
    <dt>Highland Agricultural Contracts</dt>
    <dd>Direct farm-level sourcing contracts with agricultural cooperatives in Lembang (West Java), Kopeng/Dieng (Central Java), and Batu (East Java), bypassing wholesale middlemen and cutting procurement costs by 12–15%.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-5">
  <h2>Slide 30 Budgeting &amp; Map Architecture</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Sub-Tab 1: Capital Budgeting Plan</dt>
    <dd>Total CapEx of Rp 450.0B (~Rp 90.0B/year): 50% for 50 Hyfresh fit-outs (Rp 225.0B), 30% for 45 dark store retrofits (Rp 135.0B), 20% for cold DC &amp; IT logistics (Rp 90.0B). 100% internally funded via Rp 180B lease savings, Rp 150B operational cash flow, and Rp 120B divestments. Zero net debt.</dd>
    <dt>Sub-Tab 2: Store Rollout Expansion Map</dt>
    <dd>Interactive SVG map displaying the 50 new Hyfresh supermarkets and 45 dark store retrofits across 5 clusters: Jabodetabek (22 stores, 18 hubs, Rp 99.0B), West Java (8 stores, 7 hubs, Rp 36.0B), Central Java (8 stores, 5 hubs, Rp 36.0B), East Java (8 stores, 8 hubs, Rp 36.0B), and Outer/Bali (4 stores, 7 hubs, Rp 18.0B). Dynamic inspector and bottom 5-cluster grid eliminate vertical whitespace.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-6">
  <h2>Workbook Model (Matahari.xlsx)</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Financial Baseline &amp; Modeling</dt>
    <dd><code>FINANCIALS</code> sheet: 2019–2020 audited financial statements, liquidity shortfall formulas, and debt structure schedules.</dd>
    <dt>Strategic Matrices in Excel</dt>
    <dd><code>CPM</code> (6 companies, weights summing to 1.00), <code>EFE</code> (2.68), <code>IFE</code> (2.36), <code>SWOT</code> (8 clusters), <code>SPACE</code> (-1.0, -0.6), <code>BCG</code> (format market share vs industry growth), <code>IE</code> (Total MPPA Division IV, Hyfresh Cell II), <code>GRAND</code> (Quadrant III &amp; II), <code>QSPM</code> (Row 66: Strategy 1 = 6.620 vs Strategy 2 = 5.460), and <code>CAPEX</code> (5-year Sources &amp; Uses allocation model).</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-7">
  <h2>Codebase Architecture &amp; Git Status</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Remote Repository</dt>
    <dd><code>git@github.com:faglyramschie7/group7portfolio.git</code> (Branch: <code>main</code>).</dd>
    <dt>Key Files Modified</dt>
    <dd><code>mppa-case-study.html</code> (Main interactive presentation), <code>assets/translations-id.js</code> (Indonesian dictionary), <code>HANDOFF.md</code> (Standalone markdown handoff guide).</dd>
    <dt>Testing &amp; Verification</dt>
    <dd>Zero console errors, verified in headless Chromium via Playwright tests at 1920×1080 resolution in both English and Indonesian modes.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="handoff-section-8">
  <h2>Copyable AI Prompt for New Computer</h2>
  <div class="handoff-prompt-box">
    <p><strong>Copyable Prompt to continue work in a new AI conversation:</strong></p>
    <blockquote>
      Continue working on the MPPA (PT Matahari Putra Prima Tbk) Corporate Strategy Case Study (FY2020 baseline, 2021–2025 planning horizon) by Group 7. All 30 slides of the presentation are fully implemented in `mppa-case-study.html` following Fred R. David’s strategic management framework: Stage 1 (CPM, EFE 2.68, IFE 2.36), Stage 2 (TOWS, SPACE Defensive, BCG, IE, GSM), Stage 3 (QSPM: Strategy 1 Hyfresh TAS 6.620 vs Strategy 2 TAS 5.460), and Stage 4 (Recommendations &amp; Capital Budgeting). Slide 30 features a dual-tab architecture: Tab 1 has an audit-grade Sources &amp; Uses Capital Budgeting model (Rp 450B CapEx, 100% self-funded via PSAK 73 lease savings, operating EBITDA, and non-core divestments with zero bank debt preserving BNI covenants); Tab 2 features an interactive Geospatial Store Rollout Map (50 Hyfresh supermarkets &amp; 45 dark stores across 5 regional clusters along the Java Economic Spine + 3 Central Cold-Chain DCs). Bilingual support (EN/ID) is 100% active. Read `HANDOFF.md` and the Research Handoff view in `mppa-case-study.html`. Do not introduce made-up financial terms or unbacked data. Maintain zero console errors and strict corporate finance standards.
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>
