  const ownershipSlide = slides.length;
  slides.push({ label: 'Company ownership', short: 'Ownership', html: '' });
  const mapSlide = slides.length;
  slides.push({ label: "2020 ownership map", short: "Ownership map", html: "" });
  // Keep the existing slide IDs used by the interactive controllers.
  const slideOrder = slides.map((_, index) => index).filter(index => index !== 13 && index !== ownershipSlide && index !== mapSlide);
  slideOrder.splice(3, 0, ownershipSlide, mapSlide);
  // Display IFE before EFE while retaining their IDs for slide controllers.
  slideOrder.splice(slideOrder.indexOf(19), 2, 20, 19);
  const slidePosition = computed(() => slideOrder.indexOf(currentSlide.value));
  currentSlide.value = slideOrder[slideFromUrl()] ?? slideOrder[0];
  const speakerNotes=["MPPA operates Hypermart and six other retail formats in Indonesia. The company was established in 1986 and listed in 1992. Source: case p. 587.", "MPPA expanded from supermarkets into Hypermart, then smaller neighbourhood formats. In 2018, MPPA piloted Hypermart Mobile at four Greater Jakarta stores before introducing Hyfresh in 2019. Sources: MPPA 2018 and 2023 annual reports.", "The portfolio covers broad household shopping, premium groceries, neighbourhood purchases, convenience, health and beauty, and wholesale. Source: case pp. 590\u2013592.", "Hypermart contributed about 85% of 2020 revenue, compared with 10% from Foodmart and Primo and 5% from other formats. Source: case Exhibit 5, p. 590.", "Exhibit 6 from case page 592 outlines the performance and footprint of MPPA\u2019s 7 retail formats between 2018 and 2020. The column '2020 Stores Opened/Closed' uses the notation 'X/Y', where X represents new store openings and Y represents permanent store closures during FY2020. Hypermart\u2019s '0/2' means zero new openings and two permanent closures, ending at 100 stores while anchoring 84.5% of total sales (Rp5,698 billion). Hyfresh was the only format with expansion ('2/1', net +1 store), demonstrating MPPA\u2019s strategic pivot to compact fresh supermarkets. Across the whole company, MPPA opened only 2 stores and closed 13 ('2/13'), contracting by 11 net stores to preserve liquidity during the pandemic.", "CEO Elliot Dickson sits above eight management roles. Large and small stores have separate directors. Source: case Exhibit 1, pp. 587\u2013588.", "The case identifies no published vision or mission in its reviewed sources. The quoted vision is the authors\u2019 suggested statement. Source: case p. 587.", "The background explains why the study is needed before the problem statement identifies the decision. MPPA\u2019s operating loss was Rp196.1bn in 2019, before COVID-19. The 2020 annual report attributes sales pressure to weaker purchasing power and restrictions on operating hours (p. 54), and describes online ordering, Chat & Shop, marketplace partnerships, delivery and pickup in response to weaker store traffic (p. 40). Online sales reached approximately 4\u20135% of total sales versus less than 0.1% previously. The case explicitly requests a strategic plan for 2021\u20132025 on p. 594. These facts establish the setting; they do not preselect store downsizing or digital expansion as the final strategy.", "MPPA was already loss-making before COVID-19. Revenue fell 22% in 2020; EBIT improved but remained negative at \u2212Rp140.3bn. The working-capital shortfall widened from Rp714.3bn to Rp1,227.8bn. The central challenge is to restore profitable sales through stores and digital channels within limited financial capacity. Net finance costs increased mainly because PSAK 73 added Rp166.2bn in lease finance charges; interest expense itself fell from Rp128.9bn to Rp107.3bn. This explains the weaker pretax result despite improved EBIT. Sources: case Exhibits 2\u20133; AR2020 p. 55, Notes 2.c and 28.", "Slide 9 presents the factual market footprint and food retail demand in Indonesia in 2020, drawn from Case 23 Exhibit 7 (Euromonitor, p. 593). Across the six primary staple food categories, consumer sales reached .7 billion USD. Rice (,587M) and Soft Drinks (,477M) account for over half of this volume. Furthermore, Google, Temasek, and Bain report that Indonesian e-commerce GMV expanded +54% YoY to $32B in 2020, with groceries and daily essentials being the fastest-surging category as households avoided physical crowds. This establishes that staple food demand is fundamentally non-discretionary and resilient, providing the foundational volume that accelerated into digital channels during the pandemic.", "Slide 10 analyzes consumer transaction frequency and store foot-traffic dynamics. Indonesian households purchase fresh produce, meat, dairy, and pantry staples 2 to 4 times a week (100 to 200 annual trips), whereas non-food general merchandise like appliances, homeware, or furniture are bought only once every few months or years. Perishables and packaged groceries generate over 70% to 80% of store foot-traffic. Modern hypermarkets historically used food as a traffic driver to fuel browsing in higher-margin non-food aisles. When shoppers stop visiting for groceries, sales for non-food items collapse automatically.", "Slide 11 examines category-level channel migration and the resilience of fresh perishables. Discretionary non-food merchandise faced heavy online disruption from e-commerce marketplaces like Shopee and Tokopedia, as well as specialized department stores, because ambient non-food products are easily compared and shipped. In contrast, fresh food remained physically anchored due to cold-chain infrastructure requirements. MPPA\u2019s own numbers verify this structural shift: fresh food sales contribution climbed from 24.0% in 2019 to 27.7% in 2020. This dynamic drove MPPA's strategic rollout of Hyfresh, a compact format focused 100% on groceries and fresh food rather than general merchandise.", "Slide 12 illustrates the structural format substitution that occurred in 2020. During mobility curbs, consumers didn't stop eating; they redirected weekly grocery trips away from large mall-anchored hypermarkets to nearby neighborhood minimarkets and mid-sized fresh supermarkets. Indomaret (18,113 stores) and Alfamart (15,434 stores) represent over 94% of modern food retail outlets, positioned right in residential clusters. Shoppers substituted bi-weekly destination hypermarket visits with frequent, local minimarket trips, concentrating modern food spending in proximity formats and bypassing destination malls.", "Five external factors frame the recovery challenge: restrictions on store access, weaker household consumption, permanent digital adoption for essentials, digital grocery channels, and food waste and resource efficiency. Bappenas\u2019s 2021 study provides national food-chain context for 2000\u20132019. Waste reduction could protect margin and inventory cash, but MPPA-specific losses and savings are not established. Sources: Jakarta government, BI/BPS, Google/Temasek/Bain, Bappenas and 2020 company reports.", "Jakarta\u2019s initial PSBB began on 10 April 2020. National real household consumption contracted by 2.63%. In the New Normal, 37% of Indonesian digital consumers were new adopters, and 93% intend to continue post-pandemic. Retailers offered app, website and messaging channels. Sources and survey scope are listed in Notes.", "Price rivalry and shoppers\u2019 choice of retailers pressure MPPA\u2019s sales. Its logistics network creates an entry barrier, while procurement supports its supplier position. Sources: MPPA 2020 report pp. 12, 38, 47\u201349, 115 and 126; David Chapter 3.", "Exhibit 8 shows Hypermart sales of USD414m versus USD5,900m for Indomaret and USD5,256m for Alfamart in 2020. These are selected retail-brand sales, not market shares or app sales. LotteMart Indonesia\u2019s separately reported 2020 retail-entity revenue is KRW200.3bn, approximately Rp2.47tn at 2020 average FX. All six chart bars use Rp trillion; the first five are case estimates and LotteMart is a financial-report figure. HappyFresh and Sayurbox remain unverified for comparable nationwide 2020 sales. HappyFresh is a partner-retailer platform, so its transactions can overlap retailer sales. App downloads and traffic establish activity, not annual revenue or the cause of MPPA\u2019s sales decline. Digital-rival evidence and limits are in Notes.", "Slide 18 introduces the five selected head-to-head rivals in consumer grocery retail: Indomaret, Alfamart, Transmart, Alfamidi, and LotteMart. The slide features Case Exhibit 8 (p. 593), illustrating the 2020 sales of top food retailers: Indomaret led at $5,900M, followed by Alfamart at $5,256M, Transmart at $954M, Alfa Midi at $863M, and Hypermart at $414M. The minimarket duopoly captured 72.6% of top-retailer sales, while Hypermart held a 2.7% share, highlighting the intense scale advantage of neighborhood chains.", "Slide 19 is our scored Competitive Profile Matrix across nine critical factors, summing to exactly 1.00. Alfamart leads at 3.58 and Indomaret follows at 3.47. Alfamidi scores 2.88. MPPA earns 2.46, placing it in a viable turnaround position where fresh food and Lippo ecosystem backing are assets, but standalone liquidity and hypermarket format drag constrain its score. Transmart (1.98) and LotteMart (2.10) lag behind.", "Slide 20 presents the External Factor Evaluation (EFE) Matrix for MPPA, synthesizing the 10 critical opportunities and 10 critical threats derived directly from our PESTLE, Market Footprint, and Five Forces analyses. Each factor is stated strictly as a quantified factual condition without action verbs. Total weights sum to 1.00. MPPA achieves an overall weighted score of 2.46 against the 2.50 industry midpoint, demonstrating superior agile execution in e-grocery channels and cold-chain fresh food (scores of 3 and 4), yet remaining structurally constrained by minimarket proximity ubiquity, shopping mall foot-traffic caps, and fixed PSAK 73 lease financing commitments (scores of 1 and 2). Hover over any factor row to inspect its analytical rationale and sourced corporate citation.", "Slide 21 presents the Internal Factor Evaluation (IFE) Matrix for MPPA, assessing 10 internal strengths and 10 internal weaknesses grounded in audited financial disclosures from Annual Report 2020 and Case 23. Stated strictly as diagnostic states without action verbs. Strengths receive ratings of 3 or 4, while weaknesses receive ratings of 1 or 2. Total weights equal exactly 1.00. MPPA achieves an overall weighted score of 2.21 against the 2.50 industry threshold, confirming an internally weak operational and balance sheet profile. While cold-chain fresh food and rapid omnichannel integration provide viable growth engines (Strengths score: 1.53), they are heavily weighed down by four years of chronic net losses, negative operating cash flows, mall-anchored hypermarket exposure, and PSAK 73 lease financing commitments (Weaknesses score: 0.68). Together with our EFE score of 2.46, this firmly locates MPPA in Cell VIII of the IE Matrix (Harvest or Divest/Retrenchment).", "Slide 22 represents Stage 2 (The Matching Stage) of the Fred David strategic management framework: the SWOT (TOWS) Matrix. Having established the 10 Strengths, 10 Weaknesses, 10 Opportunities, and 10 Threats in Stage 1 and evaluated them in EFE (2.46) and IFE (2.21), this matrix pairs internal capabilities with external market forces into 16 concrete actionable strategies across four distinct strategic postures: SO (Maxi-Maxi), WO (Mini-Maxi), ST (Maxi-Mini), and WT (Mini-Mini). Each strategy explicitly cites its foundational diagnostic factor codes in strict alignment with academic methodology. Key strategic strategies emerging from this synthesis include accelerating compact Hyfresh neighbourhood store conversions (SO3, ST2), monetizing under-utilized hypermarket space as digital dark-store micro-fulfillment hubs (SO4, WO1), and executing aggressive defensive retrenchment by closing chronically cash-negative mall stores to eliminate PSAK 73 lease financing liabilities (WT1, WT2). These formulated strategies populate sheet 'SWOT' in Matahari.xlsx and serve as the direct inputs evaluated in our upcoming QSPM decision matrix.", "Slide 23 synthesizes the internal and external strategic dimensions into the Strategic Position and Action Evaluation (SPACE) Matrix, plotting MPPA across Financial Position (FP: +1.80), Competitive Position (CP: -3.20), Industry Position (IP: +3.80), and Stability Position (SP: -4.60). The resulting directional vector of X = +0.60 and Y = -2.80 places MPPA in the Competitive Quadrant, directly on the frontier of the Defensive posture. This mathematical diagnosis confirms that while high structural food demand and omnichannel grocery expansion provide viable commercial runway, chronic net losses and PSAK 73 lease liabilities strictly preclude balance-sheet-heavy expansions, mandating asset-light partnerships with Grab and selective store retrenchment. Benchmarking against rivals illustrates Alfamart firmly in the Aggressive Quadrant (X: +3.80, Y: +3.00) powered by minimarket ubiquity, while Transmart languishes in the Defensive Quadrant (X: -1.60, Y: -3.60) from mall hypermarket anchor exposure.", "Slide 24 presents the Boston Consulting Group (BCG) Matrix, analyzing MPPA's multidivisional retail portfolio across Relative Market Share Position (horizontal axis, 1.0 to 0.0 with 0.50 midpoint) and Industry Sales Growth Rate (vertical axis, -20% to +20% with 0.0% midpoint). Circle areas correspond to revenue shares from Case 23 Exhibit 6. The quantitative diagnosis reveals severe corporate portfolio imbalance: 97.4% of MPPA's revenues reside in the Dog quadrant\u2014anchored by Hypermart at 84.5% (RMSP 0.41 vs Transmart's Rp13.8T in a market shrinking by -12% annually), Foodmart & Primo at 10.2% (RMSP 0.07 vs Super Indo), SmartClub at 2.0% (RMSP 0.01), and FMX at 0.7% (RMSP 0.001 vs Indomaret Rp80T). With zero divisions in Stars or Cash Cows, MPPA's growth opportunities in Question Marks\u2014Hyfresh (+10% growth) and Boston Health & Beauty (+14% growth)\u2014generate only 2.7% of total sales (Rp180B). In strict accordance with Fred David Chapter 6 matching principles, MPPA cannot sustain across-the-board store openings; leadership must aggressively harvest Hypermart's cash flow by rightsizing giant 6,000 m\u00b2 floorplates, converting surplus space into MPPA hybrid O2O storefronts, and liquidating unviable SmartClub wholesale operations to fund the rapid rollout of high-growth compact Hyfresh neighborhood supermarkets.", "Slide 25 presents the Internal-External (IE) Matrix, Fred David's nine-cell portfolio formulation model that bridges our Stage 1 evaluative inputs\u2014the IFE Matrix (weighted score of 2.21) and the EFE Matrix (weighted score of 2.46)\u2014into explicit divisional strategy formulation. The nine cells are divided into three distinct strategic regions: Region 1 (Cells I, II, IV: Grow and Build), Region 2 (Cells III, V, VII: Hold and Maintain), and Region 3 (Cells VI, VIII, IX: Harvest or Divest). At the consolidated corporate level, MPPA plots in Cell V (Hold and Maintain). However, multidivisional analysis exposes severe internal polarization across all 6 formats: 84.5% of company sales reside in Hypermart (IFE: 2.16 Average, EFE: 2.42 Medium), which borders Cell VIII (Harvest or Divest) due to giant 6,000 m\u00b2 mall leases and negative operating cash flows. Conversely, agile compact formats\u2014Hyfresh (IFE: 2.85 Average, EFE: 3.10 High) and Boston Health & Beauty (IFE: 2.75 Average, EFE: 3.15 High)\u2014land firmly in Cell II (Grow and Build), driven by high proximity demand and surging health consciousness (+10% and +14% industry growth), but represent only 2.7% of revenues (Rp180B). Meanwhile, both SmartClub (IFE: 1.65 Weak, EFE: 1.60 Low) and FMX convenience kiosks (IFE: 1.80 Weak, EFE: 1.85 Low, Rp46B, 0.7% of sales) languish in Cell IX (Harvest or Divest / Retrenchment), achieving 100% reconciliation of total portfolio sales to audited Rp6,747B across all 211 stores. In strict alignment with Fred David Chapter 6 matching principles, MPPA executes the 'Alfamart / Alfamidi Playbook': (1) Unify fragmented small supermarket storefronts under the 'Hy-' master brand (Hyfresh & Hy-Express), while preserving Primo as MPPA's dedicated luxury gourmet halo banner under the powerful 'Hy-' master brand (Hypermart & Hyfresh), eliminating sub-brand obscurity; (2) Distinctively establish Hyfresh as a standardized 500\u20131,000 m\u00b2 neighborhood fresh supermarket (60%+ fresh mix vs. minimarkets); (3) Keep physical retail storefronts active to capture foot-traffic margins while embedding MPPA hybrid O2O micro-fulfillment storefronts; and (4) Liquidate subscale non-core assets (SmartClub wholesale and unviable standalone convenience leases) to fund the rapid replication of compact Hyfresh stores in Cell II.", "Slide 26 presents the Grand Strategy Matrix (GSM), Fred David's Stage 2 matching tool evaluating MPPA across Rapid Market Growth vs. Slow Market Growth (Y-axis) and Strong Competitive Position vs. Weak Competitive Position (X-axis). MPPA Corporate Total plots in Quadrant IV (Slow Growth, Strong Position) alongside Hypermart, calling for concentric diversification and joint ventures. Hyfresh and Boston HBC operate in Quadrant II (Rapid Growth, Weak Position) where intensive turnaround strategies (market penetration and market development) are options. SmartClub and FMX occupy Quadrant III (Slow Growth, Weak Position), justifying retrenchment and divestiture.", "Slide 27 presents the Quantitative Strategic Planning Matrix (QSPM), Fred David's Stage 3 decision tool. Using weights established in the IFE and EFE matrices across 40 strategic factors, QSPM compares Strategy 1 (Accelerate Compact Fresh Storefront Rollout - Hyfresh & Rebrand under 'Hy-' Master Brand) against Strategy 2 (Downsize Large Hypermarkets into Hybrid O2O Micro-Fulfillment Storefronts). Strategy 1 achieves a Total Attractiveness Score (TAS) of 6.620, decisively outperforming Strategy 2's TAS of 5.460 by +1.160 points. Strategy 1 strongly capitalizes on consumer shifts toward fresh grocery and neighborhood shopping while escaping mall restrictions and heavy lease liabilities. Strategy 2 is retained as an operational restructuring mechanism to liberate capital for Strategy 1.", "Slide 28 synthesizes Fred David's Stage 2 matching taxonomy directly from the sample deck (Slides 19\u201329) into actionable portfolio clusters for MPPA. It organizes 9 concrete strategic alternatives across Intensive Growth (Market Penetration for Hyfresh, Market Development for transit Hy-Express, and Product Development for Value Plus private label), Defensive Restructuring (Retrenchment through hypermarket floor rightsizing, Divestiture of SmartClub wholesale and FMX kiosks, and variable lease conversions), and Supply Chain Integration (Forward O2O dark stores, Backward farm cold-chain sourcing, and Related Diversification via Boston HBC pods). This taxonomy provides the comprehensive strategic pool from which QSPM selected Strategy 1 (Hyfresh compact rollout) as the primary growth vector, while retaining Strategy 2 (hypermarket downsizing) as a self-funding defensive enabler for the Slide 29 recommendations.", "Slide 29 presents the core strategic recommendations for MPPA resulting from Fred David's Stage 3 decision tool (QSPM). Strategy 1 (Hyfresh compact fresh supermarket expansion & 'Hy-' master brand consolidation, TAS: 6.620) is chosen as the primary growth vector, capturing daily grocery foot-traffic with 35%+ fresh product share. Strategy 2 (Hypermarket floor rightsizing & hybrid O2O dark fulfillment hubs, TAS: 5.460) serves as the operational efficiency enabler, reducing excess square footage by 40-50% while scaling 60-minute delivery via GrabMart, Shopee, Tokopedia, and Hypermart Online. Strategy 3 provides balance sheet protection by restructuring long-term anchor leases under PSAK 73 to variable turnover-linked rent and liquidating subscale non-core assets (SmartClub and FMX).", "Slide 30 details the 5-Year Capital Allocation and Self-Funding Model (2021\u20132025) supporting the recommendations. Total planned CapEx of Rp450.0 billion (~Rp90.0B/year) is disciplined and phased: 45% (Rp202.5B) for 75\u2013100 new Hyfresh store fit-outs, 30% (Rp135.0B) for hypermarket rightsizing and backroom dark store automation, 15% (Rp67.5B) for cold-chain DC upgrades at Balaraja, Porong, and Cibitung, and 10% (Rp45.0B) for working capital buffers. Crucially, the transformation is 100% self-funded with zero net external bank debt: funded by +Rp120B in SmartClub/FMX divestments, +Rp180B in cumulative PSAK 73 lease cash savings (~Rp36B/yr), and +Rp150B in operational cash flows from positive-EBITDA compact stores, strictly preserving BNI credit covenants.", "Slide 31 presents the Proposed Organizational Structure for MPPA, applying Alfred Chandler's foundational thesis that 'Structure Follows Strategy' and resolving the severe governance flaws diagnosed by Fred David on case pages 587\u2013588. Under the baseline 2020 structure (Exhibit 1), Elliot Dickson faced an unsustainable span of control with eight direct reports and no COO, forcing CFO Herry Senjaya into operational firefighting. Furthermore, 'Director of Small Stores Format' lumped six disparate banners together with zero P&L accountability. The proposed structure establishes: First, a dedicated Chief Operating Officer (COO) to lead day-to-day retail and logistics execution. Second, an SBU Divisional Design by Store Format: President of Hyfresh Supermarkets directly driving QSPM Strategy 1 (50-store rollout), President of Hypermart & Rightsizing executing QSPM Strategy 2 (downsizing 45 stores into dark hubs), President of Specialty Halo Retail (Foodmart Primo and Boston), and a Head of Non-Core Divestiture Taskforce liquidating SmartClub and FMX to unlock Rp120B in cash. Third, dedicated operational enablers reporting to the COO: VP of Supply Chain & Cold Logistics (commanding the 66,755 m\u00b2 Halal DC moat) and VP of Omnichannel Commerce (scaling 30-min GrabMart/Shopee fulfillment). This provides complete, unambiguous single-thread alignment from strategic formulation directly into executive accountability.", "Slide 32 presents the Proposed Vision and Mission for MPPA formulated in strict compliance with Fred R. David Chapter 2 (2023). The Proposed Vision answers 'What do we want to become?' with a 5/5 score across all five required characteristics: Clear (identifies modern fresh food and omnichannel retail), Futuristic (establishes a 5-year target boundary by 2025), Concise (exactly 1 sentence, 30 words), Unique (anchors MPPA's turnaround around high-yield Hyfresh compact stores and Halal cold-chain logistics), and Inspiring. The Proposed Mission answers 'What is our business?' from a customer perspective in exactly 86 words raw (< 100 words), incorporating all 9 essential Fred David components: [1] Customers (Indonesian families), [2] Products/Services (nutritious farm-fresh produce, certified meats, daily groceries), [3] Markets (high-density residential neighborhoods nationwide), [4] Technology (real-time inventory routing and 30-min O2O delivery via GrabMart and Shopee), [5] Survival, Growth, and Profitability (disciplined self-funded capital budgeting protecting BNI covenants), [6] Philosophy (ethical integrity and fair-trade agricultural partnerships preventing supplier boycotts), [7] Distinctive Competence (national Halal-certified 66,755 m\u00b2 multi-temperature cold-chain network), [8] Public Image (minimizing Indonesia's Rp 330T food waste), and [9] Employees (empowering knowledgeable store staff). This statement serves as the strategic constitution ensuring every operational investment traces directly back to sustainable shareholder profit.", "Slide 33 presents the 3-Year Pro Forma Financial Statements and Strategic Financing Analysis for MPPA across 2021P–2023P, derived from Matahari.xlsx Sheet 'Projected Statements' and 'EPS_EBIT'. The model projects revenue recovering by +2% (2021), +3% (2022), and +6% (2023) to Rp 7.51T, driven by the rollout of 50 high-yield compact Hyfresh stores. Gross profit margin expands from 19.4% in 2020 to 50.0% through direct farm procurement and exiting low-margin wholesale (SmartClub). Operating expenses are held disciplined at 31% of revenue as 45 hypermarkets are rightsized, securing Rp 180B in PSAK 73 lease relief. This produces a decisive EBIT turnaround from -Rp 140B in 2020 to +Rp 1,307B in 2021 and +Rp 1,428B by 2023, halting 4 consecutive loss years (-Rp 3.53T) and generating Rp 2.50T in cumulative net income. The EPS/EBIT analysis proves that 100% internal self-funding yields the highest risk-adjusted EPS (Rp 1.53 realistic) while maintaining zero bank debt and zero equity dilution, strictly safeguarding BNI debt covenants.", "Slide 34 evaluates MPPA's firm value using Fred David's 4-method corporate valuation framework from Chapter 8, benchmarked against Indonesia's modern grocery retail market leader PT Sumber Alfaria Trijaya Tbk (Alfamart - AMRT). Method 1 (Net Worth: Stockholders' Equity minus Intangibles) values MPPA at Rp 182.4B vs AMRT's Rp 7.40T, representing a conservative tangible book value floor. Method 2 (Net Income x 5) yields -Rp 2,026.5B due to MPPA's FY2020 COVID shock loss of -Rp 405.3B, but swings to +Rp 4.35T once normalized 2023P net profit (+Rp 870.0B) is achieved. Method 3 ((Share Price / EPS) x Net Income) and Method 4 (Shares Outstanding x Share Price) both equal Rp 768.0B (7,529M shares @ Rp 102), reflecting an extreme distressed penny-stock illiquidity discount. Importantly, we provide the academic critique taught in Fred David Chapter 8 Slide 35: Method 3 is algebraically identical to Method 4, meaning averaging all four methods double-weights market capitalization (50% weight). Furthermore, averaging negative restructuring losses creates an economically artificial negative average (-Rp 77.1B) for a going concern with Rp 6.75T in annual cash sales. Applying a conservative peer P/E multiple of 15x to MPPA's 2023P earnings yields an intrinsic target valuation of Rp 13.05T (Rp 1,733/share), representing a 17.0x turnaround upside.", "Slide 35 displays the Financial Ratios Dashboard tracking 14 key metrics across Liquidity, Leverage, Operating Activity, and Profitability from Matahari.xlsx Sheet 'Ratios' across 2019A–2023P. The turnaround is proven across four pillars: First, Liquidity recovers decisively: Current Ratio expands from a distressed 0.56x in 2020 to 1.41x in 2021 and 1.97x by 2023, while Quick Ratio surges from 0.21x to 1.79x, completely ending supplier cash freezes. Second, Solvency and Debt Covenants: Long-term bank debt to equity remains at 0.1%, easily beating the BNI credit facility covenant ceiling of 250% (2.5x). Times-Interest-Earned (TIE) swings from -0.52x to +4.89x in 2021 and +5.34x by 2023, safely exceeding BNI's covenant floor of 2.0x. Third, Operating Activity: Lean inventory turnover accelerates +27% from 5.64x to 7.14x due to compact fresh perishable velocity, while fixed asset turnover reaches 9.47x. Fourth, Profitability: Gross margin expands to 50.0%, operating margin swings to +19.0%, ROA reaches 11.7%, and ROE recovers from -219.4% back to a healthy 32.3%, demonstrating total solvency restoration.", "Slide 36 synthesizes the complete strategic turnaround for PT Matahari Putra Prima Tbk (MPPA) into a phased 5-Year Implementation Roadmap (2021–2025) and delivers the boardroom conclusion. Phase 1 (2021) focuses on retrenchment and stabilization: appointing a dedicated COO, divesting non-core SmartClub and FMX to unlock Rp 120B in cash, renegotiating 15 anchor mall leases for Rp 180B in cumulative savings, and freezing bank debt to protect BNI covenants. Phase 2 (2022–2023) executes the core growth vector: opening 50 compact Hyfresh supermarkets along the Java Economic Spine, retrofitting 45 hypermarket backrooms into dark fulfillment hubs, and achieving positive operating EBIT (+Rp 1.43T). Phase 3 (2024–2025) scales omnichannel rapid delivery to 25%+ of sales, leverages the 66,755 m² central Halal cold chain moat, and resumes conservative dividend payouts. We demonstrate complete unbroken golden-thread harmony across Fred David's 4 stages: Stage 1 input matrices exposed lease vulnerabilities and fresh grocery growth; Stage 2 matching tools (SPACE Defensive, BCG Dog/Star) informed our recommendations for retrenchment and selective expansion; Stage 3 QSPM selected Hyfresh compact rollout (TAS 6.620); and Stage 4 translated this into executive COO governance, internal self-funding, balanced pro forma statements, and restored shareholder value. The strategy is ready for unanimous boardroom adoption."];
